public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * For each lead, returns the Id of the first contact (in `contacts` order)
     * whose email matches the lead's email, ignoring case and leading/trailing
     * whitespace. Returns null when the lead has a blank email or no contact matches.
     *
     * The returned list has the same size and order as `leads`.
     *
     * This method performs no SOQL and no DML; it only reads the in-memory lists.
     * It builds an email->Id map once (O(C)) then does one lookup per lead (O(L)),
     * so it stays well within the synchronous CPU limit even at 3,000 x 3,000.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> results = new List<Id>();

        // Defensive: nothing to match.
        if (leads == null) {
            return results;
        }

        // Build a lookup of normalized email -> first contact Id (in contacts order).
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact contact : contacts) {
                String email = normalize(contact == null ? null : contact.Email);
                // First contact in order wins: only store if not already present.
                if (email != null && !contactIdByEmail.containsKey(email)) {
                    contactIdByEmail.put(email, contact.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // One lookup per lead.
        for (Lead lead : leads) {
            String email = normalize(lead == null ? null : lead.Email);
            // Blank email never matches; get() returns null when there is no match.
            results.add(email == null ? null : contactIdByEmail.get(email));
        }

        return results;
    }

    /**
     * Trims surrounding whitespace and lower-cases the email for comparison.
     * Returns null for blank/null input so blank emails never match.
     */
    private static String normalize(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

## What changed and why

The original nested-loop version was correct but O(L × C). At the upper bound (3,000 leads × 3,000 contacts with no matches) that's ~9,000,000 iterations, which exceeded the 10s synchronous CPU time limit inside the import transaction — exactly what `importOf3000` caught.

The fix replaces it with the map-based approach I'd flagged as my preferred production design:

- **O(L + C) instead of O(L × C)** — one pass to build `Map<String, Id>` keyed by normalized email, one lookup per lead. A few thousand iterations total, so CPU time is a non-issue.
- **Same observable behavior:** case- and whitespace-insensitive matching (`trim().toLowerCase()`), blank/null emails never match (`normalize` returns null), and **first contact in `contacts` order wins** on duplicates (I only put a key if `!containsKey`, iterating contacts in order).
- **Same size and order** as `leads` — one `results.add(...)` per lead.
- **No SOQL and no DML** — it only reads the in-memory lists.

The lesson confirmed: readability of a nested loop isn't worth it when the inputs can reach thousands on both sides inside a transaction with a hard CPU budget — the map is just as readable and safe.

